      "content": "i appreciate the nuance, but i'm trying to get a grip on the actual clinical \"rules of thumb\" they expect us to know. if the research shows that these populations have fundamentally different \"immune signatures,\" doesn't that imply a biological reality that we should be using for triage or risk assessment?\n\nfor instance, if a patient from an ancestry that is genetically predisposed to higher pro-inflammatory cytokine levels presents with mild symptoms, shouldnt a clinician be more proactive or \"on edge\" than they would be with a white patient presenting the same way? like, is it clinically sound to assume that a \"mild\" presentation in a certain ethnic group might actually be the precursor to a much more aggressive systemic flare due to their biological baseline? i'm just trying to see if \"biology-first\" reasoning is a valid way to approach these cases.",
